# exg-c

A local 8-channel EXG host written in C. One tree, three places it runs:

| Where | What you get |
|-------|----------------|
| **Linux** | `./np-exg` — SDL2 window, `/dev/ttyUSB*` / `/dev/ttyACM*` |
| **Android** | `exg-c.apk` — same host, touch UI, USB Host (FTDI Knight) |
| **Embedded** | `nplearn/` — no-heap matcher for the MCU next to the ADS1299 |

Plug in a Knight-class board, watch the traces, clean the line noise, map
channels on a cube, save a profile. Not a medical device. Not affiliated
with NeuroPawn.

## Linux

Needs libSDL2 and membership in `dialout` (or equivalent).

```
sudo apt install build-essential libsdl2-dev pkg-config
sudo ./install-usb-permissions.sh   # udev rule for FTDI 0403:6001
make
./np-exg
```

Headless:

```
./np-exg --cli --port /dev/ttyUSB0 --seconds 8
```

`make` uses `pkg-config --libs sdl2` when it is installed, otherwise the
Debian amd64 SDL2 soname. Tests: `make test` (mock) and `make test-live`
(5 s on `/dev/ttyUSB1`, desk, not cortex).

Profiles: `~/.config/exg-c.ini` and `~/.config/exg-c/profiles/<name>.ini`.
Learn blobs: `~/.config/exg-c.learn`.

Desktop entry: `np-exg.desktop` (put `np-exg` on `$PATH`, or edit `Exec=`).

## Android

Same C host as Linux. UI is the same layout with larger hit targets.
Serial is Android USB Host (Knight FTDI `0403:6001`, plus CH340 / CP210x /
CDC ACM). Profiles and learn files live in the app files dir.

```
# Android SDK + NDK + cmake + javac on $PATH (or $ANDROID_HOME)
./android/build.sh
adb install -r android/exg-c.apk
```

Or `make android`. First run fetches SDL2 into `android/third_party/`
(gitignored). Default ABI is `arm64-v8a`.

On the phone: grant USB when the Knight is plugged in, tap **Connect**.
Do not spam Disconnect/Connect — that resets the Nano. Type-C must be in
**USB host** mode (OTG). Details: [android/README.md](android/README.md).

## Embedded (MCU)

`nplearn/` is a no-heap C library. No FILE, no threads. The Linux and
Android hosts link it; a Knight firmware image compiles only the two
core files:

```
cc -c nplearn/src/nplearn.c nplearn/src/nplearn_filt.c -Inplearn/include
```

Skip `nplearn_posix.c` on the MCU. Typical loop:

```
npl_prep(wave[ch], &rms[ch], adc_uv, n, 125.f, 50.f);
npl_add(&L, "fist", wave, rms, mask);
npl_set_cube(&L, slot, cube512);
npl_score(&L, wave, rms, mask);
n = npl_export(&L, blob, sizeof blob);   /* write to flash */
npl_import(&L, blob, n);
```

See [nplearn/README](nplearn/README). BrainCube plugins on a host include
`include/np_cube.h` (`np_virt_claim` / `np_virt_write`). Interior cells
only — the headset shell is reserved.

## First session

1. Click **Connect** (or wait — it tries the first USB port on start).
2. Leave the headset **off the head**. Click **NOISE**, then **OK**.
   That is the desk / open-input plate, not EEG.
3. Put the headset on. Sit still. Click **CALM**.
4. Leave **CLN** on. The plot is leftover after that plate + calm DC.
5. Open **Cube** (`b`). **viz** is the crimson sample cube (crimson look).
   **map** assigns 10-10 sites. `v` toggles. In map: `1`–`8` pick a channel,
   arrows find a site, Enter **Assign**. `+` / `-` zoom.
6. Open **Settings**. Type a profile name (for example `motor`) and
   click **Save**. **Load** brings it back later.
7. On Cube or Settings, click **algo …** to pick how each cell becomes
   0 or 1 (detect, sign, mean, energy, delta, fold, proton). Optional.
   Learn Record stores the whole 8³ matrix with that fold.

## What the tabs do

| Tab | Use it for |
|-----|------------|
| **Main** | Live traces, FFT, learn/match, NOISE / CALM / CLEAN |
| **Cube** | 8×8×8 state matrix. Shell = headset sites. Inside = IMU (and plugins). Drag to spin. Click a channel, then a named cell, to move it. |
| **Settings** | UI size, window, channel colors, **Save / Load / next** profiles |

Default montage: ch1–8 → Fp1 Fp2 C3 C4 P3 P4 O1 O2.

## Profiles

A profile is the whole setup: UI scale, window size, notch / high-pass,
CLEAN, gain, colors, channel on/RLD, and electrode sites.

- Linux last session: `~/.config/exg-c.ini`.
- Named files: `~/.config/exg-c/profiles/<name>.ini`.
- Android: the same names, under the app files directory.
- **next** walks saved names; then click **Load**.
- Names: letters, digits, `-`, `_`. Enter in the name box also Saves.

## Keys

`c` connect · `d` disconnect · `r` record CSV · space pause · `1`–`8`
channel (on Cube: pick that ch) · `m` Main · `s` Settings · `b` Cube ·
arrows scroll the sidebar / on Cube step 10-10 · `+` `-` zoom · Enter
assign · `q` quit

On Android those are also on-screen buttons. Touch-drag spins the cube.

## Cleaning the line (honest)

NOISE is ~8 s and a Welch plate. CLEAN destroys bins that match that
plate, then the line lock, then calm DC. CALM is worn-still. Leftover
is what Learn can store.

Default plot is ±200 µV. After NOISE, a channel still at that baseline
is drawn **FROZEN** (stops scrolling), like the official host with the
headset off. It moves again when RMS leaves the plate.

60 Hz at 125 SPS is two samples per cycle, so that notch is wide on
purpose. It still cuts line.

USB **keeps streaming** when the analog switches are off. Those
switches only route the front-end. Open inputs sit at the ADS1299 rail
and are labeled open/rail, not EEG.

## Learn

Type a name → **Record 2s** → hold still. MATCH scores live vs saved
poses. Same name overwrites. `del` removes the selected chip. Linux
samples stay in `~/.config/exg-c.learn`. Learn always runs hp 2 Hz, a
40 Hz low-pass, and the mains notch.

## Build / test

```
make            # libnplearn.a + ./np-exg
make test       # mock parser, CAL, AUTO, cube, profiles
make test-live  # 5 s collect on /dev/ttyUSB1 (desk, not cortex)
make lib        # matcher only
make android    # android/exg-c.apk (needs the Android SDK)
```
